---
description: Orchestrate non-trivial feature delivery and debugging with focused planning, evidence, bounded delegation, verification, and review.
alwaysApply: true
---

Read `docs/agent-context.md` before planning or editing. Read
`docs/harness-evolution.md` when it exists and the work reveals a recurring
agent or delivery failure.

Own the final integrated result. Handle small, obvious, localized changes
directly without subagents. For ambiguous or cross-cutting work, investigate
current behavior, resolve material product or architecture choices, create a
dependency-aware plan with acceptance criteria, and delegate independent
bounded tasks when agents are available.

{{WORKFLOW_POLICY}}

Use the strongest available model for clarification, planning, architecture,
integration, and final review. Use faster capable agents for targeted
exploration, bounded implementation, and verification. Parallel writers must
have explicit, non-overlapping file ownership. Keep integration with the parent,
inspect all returned work and the final diff, and run repository checks. Run
model reviews only when opted in. Treat agent reports as evidence to verify.
Minimize total tokens and latency: delegate adaptively, keep work with
the parent when a handoff would cost more than the task, share only needed
context, reuse compact findings, and stop obsolete branches early.

Use a proportionate risk tier: make small localized, low-risk changes directly
with a targeted check; plan and run deterministic checks for module-level
feature work; use bounded delegation and broader deterministic checks for
cross-module, data, authorization, migration, or external-API work; require
explicit human acceptance criteria for security, billing, privacy, destructive,
or production-impacting work. Prefer the fast checks in `agent-context.md` before
integration. AI review complements tests, linters, type checks, and structural
checks; it does not replace them.

Preserve unrelated changes. Do not commit unless requested. Never push, deploy,
merge, publish, alter external systems, or perform destructive actions without
explicit authorization.

Finish with one consolidated handoff: outcome, decisions and assumptions, files
changed, deterministic and behavioural evidence, review findings, checks not
run with reasons, unresolved risks, and required user action.
